WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.313
Scenario: A message uses your real project name and

2
00:00:03.313 --> 00:00:06.626
mentions a current deadline. It asks you to open

3
00:00:06.626 --> 00:00:09.939
a shared document and sign in again, but the

4
00:00:09.939 --> 00:00:13.252
sender domain differs from the partner’s known domain. Safest

5
00:00:13.252 --> 00:00:16.564
response: Option A: Verify the request with the partner

6
00:00:16.564 --> 00:00:19.877
using a previously established contact channel, and access the

7
00:00:19.877 --> 00:00:23.190
project system independently. Use relationship and workflow context as

8
00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.503
signals, but authenticate through an independent channel. Option B

9
00:00:26.503 --> 00:00:29.816
is unsafe because Changing devices does not make a

10
00:00:29.816 --> 00:00:33.129
deceptive link or credential prompt trustworthy. Option C is

11
00:00:33.129 --> 00:00:36.442
unsafe because A response goes to the potentially compromised

12
00:00:36.442 --> 00:00:39.755
sender and does not independently verify the request. Option

13
00:00:39.755 --> 00:00:43.067
D is unsafe because Public or compromised information can

14
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:46.380
make targeted deception convincing; context alone is not authentication.

15
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.693
In practice: Pause the sign-in request, contact the partner

16
00:00:49.693 --> 00:00:53.006
through known contact details, and report the mismatch to

17
00:00:53.006 --> 00:00:56.319
the security team. Key takeaway: Targeted content can be

18
00:00:56.319 --> 00:00:59.632
accurate and still fraudulent; independently verify the sender and

19
00:00:59.632 --> 00:01:00.000
workflow.
